// skip_to_next_descriptor -- operation model §4, level: end-to-end.
//
// completes_when: the nd qualifier has been delivered with the required
//                 timing -- asserted and held at least two cycles ahead of the
//                 next request. The device's internal descriptor advance is
//                 NOT part of this operation's completion; it is observed
//                 downstream, in the next request_chunk() and, with SZ_WB set,
//                 via read_descriptor_residual().
// waits for:      cycles. Never a driver state -- dma_nd_i is pin-only, with
//                 no register bit anywhere in §4, so software cannot invoke
//                 this at all.
//
// Parameterless, and therefore suspicious under the usual granularity test. It
// survives because the TIMING is the operation: nd must lead req by at least
// two cycles, and no consumer can express that as an argument to
// request_chunk().
//
// The same reasoning settles what looked like a weak completion contract: this
// operation is not software-reachable, so nothing in software ever waits on
// it. Scoping completion to qualifier delivery is both implementable and
// sufficient. Had it been software-reachable it would have needed the
// ARS treatment -- a concurrent operation supplying the terminating event.
//
// nd is left ASSERTED on return. The following request_chunk(0) carries it
// into the request and clears it after the ack.
//
// The EOL case -- list ended, channel stops, CH_EN cleared -- surfaces on the
// following request_chunk(), not here.
import wb_dma_hs_pkg::*;

extend component wb_dma_hs_c {

    target function void skip_to_next_descriptor() {
        wb_dma_hs_drive_nd(chan, 1);
        wb_dma_hs_delay_cycles(nd_lead_cycles);
    }
}
